1️⃣ Delegate Definition

A delegate is a type-safe function pointer that stores a reference to a method.

It allows:
	•	passing methods as parameters
	•	callbacks
	•	event handling
	•	flexible runtime behavior

Syntax
public delegate int Operation(int a, int b);
Usage
static int Add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Operation op = Add;

int result = op(2,3);

2️⃣ Delegate as Parameter (Strategy Pattern)
Delegates allow dynamic behavior injection.

public static int Calculate(int a, int b, Operation op)
{
    return op(a,b);
}

Usage
Calculate(5,3,Add);
Calculate(5,3,Multiply);

3️⃣ Built-in Delegates
Instead of custom delegates we use Action, Func, Predicate.
Delegate          Return          Example
Action            void            logging
Func              returns value    LINQ
Predicate         bool            filtering


Action
Action log = () => Console.WriteLine("Logging");

log();

With parameter
Action<string> log = msg => Console.WriteLine(msg);

Func
Func<int,int,int> add = (a,b) => a + b;

int result = add(5,3);

Used heavily in LINQ
numbers.Select(x => x * 2);

Predicate

Used for boolean checks
Predicate<int> isEven = x => x % 2 == 0;
Example
numbers.Find(x => x % 2 == 0);

4️⃣ Lambda Expressions

Short syntax to write delegates.

Syntax
parameters => expression

Examples
x => x * 2
(a,b) => a + b

Multi-line lambda
(a,b) =>
{
    int result = a + b;
    return result;
}

Equivalent to:
int Add(int a,int b)
{
 return a + b;
}

5️⃣ Delegates in Sorting

Example
numbers.Sort((a,b) => a - b);
Sort expects comparison delegate.

Return meaning
Return Value          Meaning
< 0                   a before b
0                     equal

6️⃣ Multicast Delegates

Delegates can hold multiple methods.

notify += SendEmail;
notify += SendSMS;
notify += Log;

Execution order
SendEmail()
SendSMS()
Log()

Internally delegate keeps Invocation List.

7️⃣ Events

Events are delegates with restricted access.

Instead of:
public Notify notify;
Use:
public event Notify Completed;
External classes can only:
+= subscribe
-= unsubscribe

They cannot invoke the event.

Only the publisher class can raise it.

8️⃣ Event Example

Publisher
public class Process
{
    public event Action Completed;

    public void Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Processing");

        Completed?.Invoke();
    }
}
Subscriber
Process p = new Process();

p.Completed += SendEmail;
p.Completed += Log;


9️⃣ ASP.NET Core Real Example

Delegates power middleware pipeline.

app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine("Before request");

    await next();

    Console.WriteLine("After request");
});

Middleware internally uses delegate:
RequestDelegate(HttpContext context)
Pipeline
Middleware1 → Middleware2 → Middleware3
Each middleware calls next() delegate.


