1️⃣ Why Events Can Cause Memory Leaks

Events can cause memory leaks because the publisher holds a reference to the subscriber through the delegate invocation list. 
If the subscriber does not unsubscribe, the garbage collector cannot collect it even if the object is no longer needed.

2️⃣ Weak Events

Weak events solve the memory leak problem.

Normally events create a strong reference.
Publisher → Subscriber

With weak events, the reference becomes weak, meaning GC can still collect the subscriber.
Publisher - - - > Subscriber
         (weak reference)
If subscriber is garbage collected:
Publisher event invocation list cleaned automatically

Where Weak Events Are Used

Most common in:
	•	WPF
	•	UI frameworks
	•	long-lived publishers

Example class:
WeakEventManager
Used like:
WeakEventManager<Publisher, EventArgs>
    .AddHandler(publisher, "EventName", HandlerMethod);

When Weak Events Are Needed

Use them when:
Publisher lifetime >> Subscriber lifetime
Example:
Application service → UI component
Cache service → temporary listeners
Global event bus

Weak events use weak references so that the subscriber can still be garbage collected even if it forgets to unsubscribe from the event.

3️⃣ Event vs Observer Pattern

Many developers think they are the same, but events are actually a built-in implementation of the observer pattern.
C# events are a language-level implementation of the observer pattern. 
They allow publishers to notify multiple subscribers without knowing who they are, 
implementing the publish–subscribe communication model.


Interviewers often ask:

When would you avoid events?

Answer:

Avoid events when:
many-to-many communication
distributed systems
microservices
cross-service communication

Instead use
Message queues
Event bus
Kafka
Service Bus

C# events implement the observer pattern using delegates. However, because events create strong references between publisher and subscriber, they can cause memory leaks if subscribers do not unsubscribe. 
Weak events solve this by using weak references, allowing subscribers to be garbage collected.
